 ## Overview
-DoF-NeRF is a neural rendering framework that extends Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to simulate the depth-of-field (DoF) effects of a physical lens, allowing it to operate on and synthesize views with arbitrary focus configurations [S1]. Unlike standard NeRF variants that assume pinhole cameras or all-in-focus inputs, DoF-NeRF explicitly models the aperture and focus distance, enabling the recovery of sharp, all-in-focus 3D representations from shallow DoF inputs and the manipulation of virtual aperture and focus parameters during rendering [S1].
+DoF-NeRF is a neural rendering framework that extends standard Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to simulate the depth-of-field (DoF) effects of a physical lens, allowing the model to operate on views with different focus configurations and manipulate DoF effects by adjusting virtual aperture and focus parameters [S1]. Unlike standard NeRF, which assumes a pinhole camera and all-in-focus inputs, DoF-NeRF explicitly models the aperture and focus distance, enabling it to handle shallow DoF inputs and produce clear, all-in-focus novel view synthesis with reduced blurriness and enhanced texture details [S1]. It achieves this by combining optical models with classical volume rendering to approximate the DoF effect under arbitrary aperture size and focus distance configurations [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-DoF-NeRF modifies the volume rendering equation to incorporate geometric optics principles, specifically the Circle of Confusion (CoC), to simulate how light scatters from spatial points across an imaging plane [S1].
+DoF-NeRF extends the NeRF architecture by incorporating the representation of the Circle of Confusion (CoC) to simulate DoF. The method approximates the DoF effect through two physical steps: (1) the radiance of spatial points scattering to a particular area, and (2) the center ray accepting the scattered radiance from neighboring rays [S1]. This is implemented by combining optical models with classical volume rendering [S1].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs:** A set of $N$ input images $\mathcal{I} = \{I_1, I_2, \dots, I_N\}$ with known camera parameters (obtained via COLMAP) [S1].
-*   **Outputs:** A trained NeRF model $G_\Theta$ and learned per-image aperture parameters $\hat{K}$ and focus distances $\hat{F}$ [S1].
