 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Divergence:** Directly optimizing aperture and focus with random rays can lead to divergence or sub-optimal results due to the complexity of the concentrate-and-scatter mechanism [S1]. The two-stage approach (pinhole first) mitigates this [S1].
-*   **Computational Cost:** Computing scatter for all rays in an image is prohibitively expensive. The patch-based ray selection is a necessary compromise to manage memory and compute time [S1].
-*   **CoC Radius Calculation:** The indicator function $\mathbb{1}_{\delta_c(p) > 2 \|p - p_j\|^2}$ requires precise calculation of pixel distances and CoC diameters. Incorrect scaling of $K$ or $F$ can lead to either excessive blur (if $K$ is too large) or no DoF effect (if $K$ is too small) [S1].
+*   **Divergence:** Using random rays for the concentrate-and-scatter method in all iterations can lead to divergence or sub-optimal results due to ray gathering [S1]. The two-stage approach mitigates this [S1].
+*   **Computational Cost:** Computing rays for the whole imaging plane in each iteration is computationally unacceptable [S1]. Patch-based selection is a necessary compromise [S1].
+*   **Blurriness in Stage 1:** In Stage 1, the rendered foreground and background may be blurred due to shallow DoF inputs, as the model learns a coarse 3D representation without DoF correction [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The concentrate-and-scatter rendering is more expensive than standard volume rendering due to the neighbor aggregation step. However, the patch-based selection limits the per-iteration cost to a fraction of the image [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the NeRF model, plus $2N$ learnable parameters for $K$ and $F$ [S1].
-*   **Performance:** DoF-NeRF outperforms vanilla NeRF on shallow DoF inputs.
-    *   **Real-world dataset (amiya scene):** PSNR 28.311 (vs NeRF 26.924), SSIM 0.9289 (vs 0.9092), LPIPS 0.1370 (vs 0.1633) [S1].
-    *   **Synthetic dataset (room scene):** PSNR 29.443 (vs NeRF 26.668), SSIM 0.9135 (vs 0.8743), LPIPS 0.1502 (vs 0.1961) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Explicitly models physical optics, allowing manipulation of DoF effects and recovery of all-in-focus scenes from blurry inputs. Plug-and-play with NeRF-based frameworks [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires joint optimization of camera parameters, which can be unstable without the two-stage training. Assumes ideal optical systems (neglects ray distortions) [S1].
+*   **Performance:** On the real-world dataset (amiya scene), DoF-NeRF achieves PSNR 28.311 vs NeRF 26.924, SSIM 0.9289 vs 0.9092, and LPIPS 0.1370 vs 0.1633 [S1]. On the synthetic dataset (room scene), DoF-NeRF achieves PSNR 29.443 vs NeRF 26.668, SSIM 0.9135 vs 0.8743, and LPIPS 0.1502 vs 0.1961 [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Explicit aperture modeling enables NeRF to tackle shallow DoF inputs and benefits all-in-focus novel view synthesis, producing photo-realistic views with fine-grained details [S1]. It is plug-and-play and can be inserted into NeRF-based frameworks [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The method requires learning additional parameters (aperture and focus) per image, increasing optimization complexity [S1]. It assumes ideal optical systems, neglecting ray distortions caused by the lens [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When input images have shallow depth-of-field and the goal is to synthesize clear, all-in-focus novel views or to simulate DoF effects with adjustable aperture and focus [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When inputs are already all-in-focus and high fidelity to pinhole camera models is sufficient, as standard NeRF may be simpler and faster [S1].
 
 ## Background
-DoF-NeRF was introduced by Zijin Wu, Juewen Peng, and Weicai Zhong from Huazhong University of Science and Technology and Huawei [S1]. It was published in the ACM Multimedia Conference (MM '22) in October 2022 [S1]. The work addresses the limitation of existing NeRF variants (like RawNeRF and NeRFocus) which assume all-in-focus inputs, by incorporating geometric optics to handle shallow DoF inputs [S1].
+DoF-NeRF was introduced by Zijin Wu, Juewen Peng, and Weicai Zhong from Huazhong University of Science and Technology and Huawei [S1]. The paper was published in the ACM SIGGRAPH Conference on Computer Graphics (SIGGRAPH) in 2022, specifically in the proceedings of the 2022 ACM SIGGRAPH Conference on Computer Graphics (MM '22) held in Lisboa, Portugal [S1]. The work addresses the limitation of existing NeRF models that assume all-in-focus inputs, which limits their applicability to real-world images with finite depth-of-field [S1]. It builds upon the principles of geometric optics to extend NeRF's capability to simulate lens aperture effects [S1].
 
